  "description": "Classical mechanics at the advanced undergraduate level: Lagrangian and Hamiltonian mechanics with symmetries, constraints and the calculus of variations; rigid bodies, Euler's equations and the symmetric top; small oscillations and normal modes; canonical transformations, Poisson brackets, the Hamilton-Jacobi equation and action-angle variables; perturbation theory; fluid mechanics from the continuum equations to viscous flow; and chaos and non-linear dynamics.",
  "cosmologyNeeds": "The inflaton is a field obeying Hamilton's equations, and the slow-roll and Hubble-friction arguments of 8.286 are Lagrangian mechanics with a time-dependent damping term. The fluid equations (continuity, Euler, sound waves) are the starting point of the Jeans analysis and of linear structure formation in 8.902, and the sound waves of the photon-baryon fluid leave the acoustic peaks in the CMB. Perturbation theory and secular effects underlie the perihelion precession that general relativity explains.",
  "notes": "The source is the course's full lecture notes in seven chapters (typed, prepared for the course; no videos). OCW says they cover all 26 lectures but does not mark the lecture boundaries, so the syllabus follows the notes' sections and lecture numbers are section numbers (1.1, 1.2, ...). The notes' figures are drawn for the course; draw our own diagrams anyway. Neither the notes nor the OCW pages carry a courtesy or rights-reserved notice (checked 2026-11-19). The prerequisite 8.223 (Classical Mechanics II) is a January course.",
